{"qid":"mathvista_294","dataset":"mathvista","question":"Hint: Please answer the question requiring a floating-point number with two decimal places and provide the final value, e.g., 1.23, 1.34, 1.45, at the end.\nQuestion: In Fig. 21-25, the particles have charges $q_1=-q_2=100 \\mathrm{nC}$ and $q_3=-q_4=200 \\mathrm{nC}$, and distance $a=$ $5.0 \\mathrm{~cm}$. What is the $x$ component of the net electrostatic force on particle 3?","gold":"0.17","category":"textbook question answering","generated_text":"We are given a square with side length $a = 5.0$ cm = 0.05 m. The charges are:\n\n- $q_1 = -100$ nC = $-100 \\times 10^{-9}$ C (at top-left)\n- $q_2 = +100$ nC = $+100 \\times 10^{-9}$ C (at top-right)\n- $q_3 = -200$ nC = $-200 \\times 10^{-9}$ C (at bottom-left)\n- $q_4 = +200$ nC = $+200 \\times 10^{-9}$ C (at bottom-right)\n\nWe are to find the **x-component of the net electrostatic force on particle 3**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Identify the forces on particle 3\n\nParticle 3 is at the bottom-left corner. The forces acting on it are from:\n\n- $q_1$ (top-left) \u2014 same side, vertical distance $a$, horizontal distance 0.\n- $q_2$ (top-right) \u2014 diagonal, distance $d = \\sqrt{a^2 + a^2} = a\\sqrt{2}$\n- $q_4$ (bottom-right) \u2014 same side, horizontal distance $a$, vertical distance 0.\n\nNote: $q_3$ is the particle we are calculating force **on**, so we don't consider force from itself.\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Use Coulomb\u2019s Law\n\nCoulomb\u2019s Law: $F = k \\frac{|q_1 q_2|}{r^2}$, where $k = 8.99 \\times 10^9$ N\u00b7m\u00b2/C\u00b2.\n\nWe need the **x-component** of the net force on $q_3$. So we will compute the x-component of each force acting on $q_3$.\n\n---\n\n### Force from $q_1$ on $q_3$:\n\n- $q_1 = -100$ nC, $q_3 = -200$ nC \u2192 both negative \u2192 **repulsive** force.\n- Since $q_1$ is directly above $q_3$, the force is **downward** (along negative y-axis).\n- So, **x-component = 0**.\n\n---\n\n### Force from $q_4$ on $q_3$:\n\n- $q_4 = +200$ nC, $q_3 = -200$ nC \u2192 opposite signs \u2192 **attractive** force.\n- $q_4$ is to the right of $q_3$, so the force on $q_3$ is **toward $q_4$**, i.e., **to the right** (positive x-direction).\n\nSo, this force is entirely in the **+x direction**.\n\nCompute magnitude:\n\n$$\nF_{43} = k \\frac{|q_4 q_3|}{a^2} = (8.99 \\times 10^9) \\frac{(200 \\times 10^{-9})(200 \\times 10^{-9})}{(0.05)^2}\n$$\n\nFirst, compute numerator: $(200 \\times 10^{-9})^2 = 4 \\times 10^{-12}$\n\nDenominator: $(0.05)^2 = 0.0025 = 2.5 \\times 10^{-3}$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nF_{43} = (8.99 \\times 10^9) \\frac{4 \\times 10^{-12}}{2.5 \\times 10^{-3}} = (8.99 \\times 10^9) \\times (1.6 \\times 10^{-9}) = 8.99 \\times 1.6 = 14.384 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nSo, $F_{43,x} = +14.384$ N\n\n---\n\n### Force from $q_2$ on $q_3$:\n\n- $q_2 = +100$ nC, $q_3 = -200$ nC \u2192 opposite signs \u2192 **attractive** force.\n- $q_2$ is at top-right, $q_3$ at bottom-left \u2192 diagonal.\n\nThe vector from $q_3$ to $q_2$ is: $\\vec{r} = (a, a)$ \u2192 so direction is 45\u00b0 above positive x-axis.\n\nBut since the force is attractive, the force on $q_3$ is **toward $q_2$**, so direction is 45\u00b0 above positive x-axis.\n\nSo, the force vector has components:\n\n$$\nF_{23,x} = F_{23} \\cos(45^\\circ), \\quad F_{23,y} = F_{23} \\sin(45^\\circ)\n$$\n\nFirst, compute magnitude:\n\n$$\nF_{23} = k \\frac{|q_2 q_3|}{r^2} = (8.99 \\times 10^9) \\frac{(100 \\times 10^{-9})(200 \\times 10^{-9})}{(a\\sqrt{2})^2} = (8.99 \\times 10^9) \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-12}}{2 \\times (0.05)^2}\n$$\n\nNote: $(a\\sqrt{2})^2 = 2a^2 = 2 \\times 0.0025 = 0.005$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nF_{23} = (8.99 \\times 10^9) \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-12}}{0.005} = (8.99 \\times 10^9) \\times (4 \\times 10^{-10}) = 8.99 \\times 0.4 = 3.596 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nNow, x-component:\n\n$$\nF_{23,x} = F_{23} \\cos(45^\\circ) = 3.596 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\approx 3.596 \\times 0.7071 \\approx 2.545 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Total x-component of force on $q_3$:\n\n$$\nF_{net,x} = F_{43,x} + F_{23,x} = 14.384 + 2.545 = 16.929 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Final Answer:\n\nFinal answer: 16.93","n_input":237,"n_generated":1505,"seq_len":1742,"grid":[9,8],"img_span":[4,76],"user_text_span":[77,232],"answer_seg":[1498,1504],"ans_tp":[1734,1735,1736,1737,1738,1739],"texts":["<|im_start|>","user","\n","<|vision_start|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|image_pad|>","<|vision_end|>","Hint",":"," Please"," answer"," the"," question"," requiring"," a"," floating","-point"," number"," with"," two"," decimal"," places"," and"," provide"," the"," final"," value",","," e",".g",".,"," ","1",".","2","3",","," ","1",".","3","4",","," ","1",".","4","5",","," at"," the"," end",".\n","Question",":"," In"," Fig","."," ","2","1","-","2","5",","," the"," particles"," have"," charges"," $","q","_","1","=-","q","_","2","=","1","0","0"," \\","mathrm","{n","C","}$"," and"," 